Martin Read schrieb:
> Klaus Kassner <Klaus.Kassner@physik.uni-magdeburg.de> wrote:

>>And then there is the old argument by Boudewijn.  The reason for the 
>>wand to exist with 100% probability is that you may just not be able to 
>>get an absolutely necessary item by chance, such as candles, or one that 
>>is at least close to absolutely necessary, such as a ring of levitation 
>>(or at least boots).  So you may need to burn one wish for something 
>>"absolutely" necessary.  If you don't have more than one, where is the 
>>reward for cleaning out the castle?

> Levitation is only moderately close to necessary.  There are other ways
> to get back across the Medusa, Castle, and Juiblex levels; waterwalking
> (and there is a *guaranteed* pair of boots of water walking in Vlad's
> tower), ice magic, and boulders all spring to mind.  On the way *down*

I am not talking about that at all.  In my opinion, levitation is 
necessary on the levels of air and water, and extremely useful on fire. 
  How would you ever get to the portal on air without being able to 
control your movements?  I am not saying it is impossible, but it turns 
levitation from "moderately necessary" to "close to absolutely 
necessary"...  Clearly, within the main dungeon, you can get by without 
levitation.

> the dungeon, you can get past Medusa and Juiblex by means of digging
> magic (or even a pick-axe).

> And the castle already contains four storerooms and four dragons, which
> are quite a reward in themselves.

Hardly.  By the time I get to the castle, I almost never need anything 
from the storerooms.  If all the armour there was magical, it would be a 
different story.  But it is extremely rare you get from there what you 
need.  (Happened to me once or twice in ~30 ascensions.)  The dragons 
are hardly useful either.  The only resistance that I often haven't got 
by that time is disintegration.  So, again, if the dragons were 
guaranteed to be all black or all black or gray, it would be a different 
story.

> David's proposal is that (a) wands of wishing should be unwrestable and
> unrechargeable and (b) the Castle wand should *always* have three wishes
> in it when found.

Yes, except I was not talking about his proposal but rather about his 
general argument (see below).  Also, I am just saying that five 
guaranteed wishes don't seem too many for me, but I would also put up 
with three guaranteed ones.

>>And finally, I agree also with David's argument that the game should be 
>>winnable without (too much) help by the RNG.  In fact, I tend to be 
>>purist in that respect -- I believe that there should be essentially no 
>>games that are lost due to the RNG.

> This became an impossible desire when the ability for monsters to use
> items was introduced.  Even a wand of fire or cold can kill a first
> level character.

Or a trap.  Or a poisoned arrow.  But note that I said "essentially no" 
games that are lost due to the RNG.  I don't care too much about a one 
percent chance to lose an early character by bad luck.  Since there is 
no way for me to have survival rates close to 99%, I am not *that* 
purist.  On the other hand, a 1% chance to die after the castle does 
bother me, because I have a 100% score in that respect.  None of my 
characters died after the castle (I mean irreversibly, there were two 
deaths with recovery by an amulet).  By the castle, you have had so much 
time and opportunities to prepare your character for every incident that 
this simply should not happen.  So if there are any chances of 
instadeath for a character into which so much time has been invested, 
the game should (IMHO) also provide for the means to avoid them by care, 
irrespective of the RNG. It does so, I believe, with the possible 
exception of Pestilence...
